--TEST--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` takes `spawn script`'s operand and refuses a request that arrived on no connection, rather than answering as though a peer were attached — ADR 0083 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0083 § 2 makes the operand `spawn script`'s, so a path is one of the two
// forms the entry may take and the compiler accepts this line. What is behind
// it is § 1's root isolate, prepared by this member and started by the
// connection -- and a script run from the command line is not a connection, so
// there is no slot to leave one in. The alternative is the one this case
// exists to refuse: answering `void` and leaving a program to believe it had
// upgraded.
try {
    Core\Socket::upgrade("sockets/chat.nvs");
    echo "upgraded\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The second parameter is optional, and omitting it above is not what was
// refused -- an entry declaring no parameter needs no `args:`, exactly as
// `spawn script` with no `with(args:)` clause does. So the same report arrives
// either way, which is what says the refusal is about the connection and not
// about the call's shape.
try {
    Core\Socket::upgrade("sockets/chat.nvs", args: {room: "lobby"});
    echo "upgraded\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "refused again\n";
}
--EXPECT--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` needs a connection to upgrade and this request arrived on none: only a request a server framed an upgrade for is offered one
refused again
